{
  "id": "u03-l01",
  "unit": "u03-family",
  "title": "Parents & siblings",
  "status": "draft",
  "tip": "Two pairs for parents: **মা / বাবা** (*ma / baba*) are universal; **আম্মু / আব্বু** (*ammu / abbu*) are the warm, everyday words in many Bangladeshi (especially Muslim) homes — like 'mom' and 'dad'. In Kolkata and Hindu families you'll mostly hear মা and বাবা. Both pairs are correct: use whichever your family uses.\n\nSiblings are simply **ভাই** (*bhai*, brother) and **বোন** (*bon*, sister). Remember: no word for 'is' and no 'a / the': **ও আমার ভাই** = 'He (is) my brother.'\n\nShowing someone a photo? Start with **এটা আমার …** 'this is my …': **এটা আমার পরিবার** = 'This is my family.' (**পরিবার**, *poribar* = family.) Bangladeshi families are often large and close-knit, and grandparents, uncles and aunts may live under the same roof — which is why the next lessons have so many kinship words.",
  "vocab": [
